WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.460
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Daniel Goleman, who is, of course,

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:10.550
well known for his writings on emotional intelligence,
on meditation, a number of other topics that

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.269
that are very interesting to me.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:13.480
It's great to have you on today.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.879
Speaker 5: Thanks, David.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:15.879
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:19.420
Speaker 1: So maybe maybe just to start the
conversation, we're hopefully starting to

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:26.300
get to the beginning of the end or the end
of the middle or some somewhere closer to

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.170
where life will in some ways return to normal
vis a vis the pandemic.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:34.930
But one of the things I've been talking with
many people about are kind of speculating

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.950
about what the longer term effects might be
of this pandemic.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:44.300
I think certainly some degree of of the feeling
that the world is a hostile place which may

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.000
connect to some agoraphobia for many people.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.790
People may be realizing that the risks that
they were taking before were bigger than maybe

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:55.790
they acknowledged.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.010
And changing behavior going forward.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.930
It's just some ideas.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:06.360
How are you conceiving of this kind of exit
process and what the impacts may be emotionally,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.360
psychologically?

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.500
Speaker 5: Well, I see two upsides coming
at some point.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.280
One is that.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:20.150
Everybody's been under tremendous stress because
of the lockdown, particularly working parents.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.420
I mean, how do you work from home and manage
kids running in and out of your room and,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.920
you know, life goes on.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.810
And that, just as I realize, is the stress
level to a point where people may be feeling

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:40.390
emotionally exhausted and that hopefully will
be relieved when things ease up.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.240
The second is a kind of disconnect.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:50.729
We haven't been able to see our friends hanging
out in the way we usually do and zoom or Skype

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:58.310
these video conferencing ways of meeting people
do something a little weird to the social

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.729
circuitry of the brain.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:04.630
And that is I can't look you in the eye at
the same time.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.200
I see your face
Speaker 1: if you're right there, either looking

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.840
at the camera, if you look at the camera,
it looks like you're looking at me, but then

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.390
you're not really looking at where you see
me on your screen.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.250
Speaker 5: Exactly.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:21.860
So the disconnect is that there's no eye contact
anymore, at least on screen, and we'll be

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.090
able to have that again.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.020
What a relief to to the extent we can see
people.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.510
And you're right, people still have to be
safe.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:29.510
I'm double vaccinated.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.010
And we just saw some friends outside still
with masks.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.130
You know, I'm of an age where I might be vulnerable.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:41.370
I've got to be very careful and people need
to be careful to not just for themselves,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.550
but for other people they love and contact
regularly.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:50.981
But then the big unanswered question is the
one you raise, what will be the long term

00:02:50.981 --> 00:02:59.500
consequences of having not been able to see
people, as usual, I think, on children and

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:04.630
teenagers, because the social and emotional
circuitry of the brain is the last part of

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.810
the brain to become anatomically mature.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:11.520
It doesn't get set in place until the mid
20s.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.069
And at every stage, children and teens need
something.

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:23.760
There's what's called a five to seven shift
when kids prefrontal cortex comes online and

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:31.660
circuitry that allows them to just say no,
the emotional impulse gets stronger and stronger.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:38.209
But then as they develop, they need to be
with other kids who the kids learned an immense

00:03:38.209 --> 00:03:42.440
amount emotionally and socially from their
peers.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.790
And all of a sudden it's gone and we don't
know what the consequences would be.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:47.860
I tend to be an optimist.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:54.660
I tend to think, well, you know, kids are
really adept at remedial learning and I hope

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:55.790
that turns out to be true.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:59.410
We just don't know
Speaker 1: yet from from a strictly mathematical

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:05.800
standpoint, a 30 year old being home for a
year and a 60 year old being home for a year

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.640
as a proportion of their lives is very different
than someone that was six and from six to

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:12.640
seven.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.940
That's that's more than 15 percent of their
lives.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:22.919
Is that kind of relative comparison important
in thinking about who might suffer the biggest

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:23.919
impact?

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:26.999
In a sense,
Speaker 5: perhaps what that mathematics does

00:04:26.999 --> 00:04:33.820
not factor in is the rate at which children
might be able to make up for what they didn't

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:34.820
get.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:41.350
And, you know, you see this children or children's
brains are remarkably plastic that way.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:50.680
So we have a model of the family unit to parents,
you know, when a kid or kids as being essential.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.050
But actually the human brain was designed
for all kinds of tragedies.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.319
Maybe your parents get killed.

00:04:56.319 --> 00:04:59.349
You know, maybe someone else takes care of
you.

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:03.419
You can learn the same things from the caretaker
that you would have from your parent.

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:04.949
That's the way the brain is designed.

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:12.770
So we'll see how adept kids are at undoing
what has been done to them.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:18.249
Speaker 1: One of the things I've been noticing
myself is now is someone who's double vaccinated.

00:05:18.249 --> 00:05:22.289
And I'm following all the CDC guidelines,
which now include that unless you're in a

00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:24.869
big crowd outdoors, you don't have to wear
a mask.

00:05:24.869 --> 00:05:28.999
President Joe Biden telling us this, the CDC
telling us this last year.

00:05:28.999 --> 00:05:33.809
But yet I still find myself thinking, even
though I know I'm following all the guidelines,

00:05:33.809 --> 00:05:39.300
when someone else goes by without a mask,
there's a second where my mind does something

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:42.999
where I say, whoa, hold on, this is this is
dangerous in some way.

00:05:42.999 --> 00:05:45.119
But then I remember hold on a second.

00:05:45.119 --> 00:05:49.589
At the worst case, one out of a thousand cases
were being transmitted outdoors.

00:05:49.589 --> 00:05:51.280
The person's 20 feet away.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.330
I've been conditioned in a sense to perceive
danger, maybe where there is not.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.339
Is that something that you expect will persist
for some time?

00:06:00.339 --> 00:06:06.229
Speaker 5: Well, David, you're very probabilistically
oriented.

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:07.499
Yes.

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:10.469
And that's kind of a cognitive control.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:12.129
You're reframing the situation.

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:13.870
I completely agree.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.860
And not many people can do that.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:21.080
Lots of people just react viscerally, they
don't have the cognitive wherewithal to to

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:25.330
say, well, it's one of a thousand and it's
outdoors and so on.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:32.800
So I think people will be reacting now and
for some time from their emotional centers

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.919
rather rashly and cognitively.

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:39.610
Speaker 1: You've been doing more and more
work, or at least I've been seeing more and

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:43.479
more of your work related to meditation of
late.

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:47.849
And I've been studying with great interest
some of the research that is done into the

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:51.029
effects of meditation on the brain.

00:06:51.029 --> 00:06:55.409
And there are all sorts of different opinions
about is the effect overstated or understated

00:06:55.409 --> 00:07:00.360
or do we not yet really know whether in the
context of the pandemic or not?

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:05.099
Can you talk a little bit about your focus
on on meditation and why you think it's an

00:07:05.099 --> 00:07:07.520
important thing for in particular now?

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.440
Speaker 5: Well, I think it's a natural next
step from emotional intelligence.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.139
Emotional intelligence, as in my mind, has
four parts.

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:20.259
There's self-awareness and then using that
awareness to manage disturbing emotions well

00:07:20.259 --> 00:07:22.330
and marshal positive ones.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:26.669
Well, it turns out that meditation is a very
good tool for doing that.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:29.909
It is an exercise in self-awareness.

00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:36.360
And I just did a book with Richard Davidson,
who's a neuroscientist at University of Wisconsin,

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.250
and we looked at the very best studies we
found from the beginning.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:45.550
People tend to be more calm and more clear
if they meditate and there's a dose response

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.319
effect so that the longer you do it, the more
hours you put in, the more this seems to happen.

00:07:51.319 --> 00:07:58.550
Long term meditators are triggered less often,
less severely and recovered more quickly.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:06.979
We found from the data and very high Olympic
level meditators are a whole other thing.

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:12.979
And it turns out that if you do an exercise
that increases warm heartedness, compassion

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:16.899
or caring, you become more compassionate,
caring.

00:08:16.899 --> 00:08:18.089
It's kind of a no brainer.

00:08:18.089 --> 00:08:23.349
But it turns out that the part of the brain
actually that.

00:08:23.349 --> 00:08:28.919
Were those circuits reside, become stronger
and that behaviorally you're more likely to

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:29.979
help someone in need.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:32.180
You're more generous and so on.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:34.380
So meditation seems to work.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.640
And the second part of emotional intelligence
is empathy.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.020
And I'm I'm rethinking emotional intelligence
now.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.030
Can I mention my podcast, please?

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:47.310
Of course it's available.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:54.210
I just started a podcast called First Person
Singular, where some of my newest thinking

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:57.390
is shared with people.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:09:01.770
And one of the ways I'm rethinking it is in
terms of what is empathy.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.990
I know it's cognitive.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:10.520
I know it's emotional, but it also is empathic
concern, which means you care about the other

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:15.080
person, not just, you know, how they think,
you know how they feel, but you want what's

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.210
best for them.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.390
It's like a parent's love for a child.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:24.980
And I think this leads to compassion or to
that whole unfolding of warm heartedness.

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:28.890
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, what's very interesting
to me about this, and I don't want to make

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.740
everything political and I'm glad not to,
but one of the things that I've been I've

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:38.340
been reading quite a bit about is that on
well-designed sort of assessments of the level

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.330
of empathy and compassion that are triggered
in people when they are told the particular

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:49.520
story or see a particular image, it can very
accurately predict one's political leanings

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:55.580
on a sort of general conservative liberal
spectrum, as we might imagine it in the United

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.730
States applies to Democrats and Republicans.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:01.350
And then the next question sort of becomes
how much of this is nature?

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:02.350
How much is nurture?

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:06.360
How much is the environment you grew up around
and kind of what your parents modeled for

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.350
you versus is there something in the brain?

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.980
And different people have different opinions.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:16.590
In theory at least, this could be very relevant
from a policy standpoint, could it not?

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:22.320
Speaker 5: You know, it reminds me of some
data I saw years ago when I was a science

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:30.290
journalist at The New York Times, and it was
a behavioral genetic study of different behavioral

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:36.350
variables, one of which was conservatism,
which turned out to have a very high heritability,

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:45.790
which suggests that part of this may be in
our inherited circuitry, the tendency to want

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:52.890
things to be safe, to want things to be the
same, and then translate that into a political

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:53.890
orientation.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:58.780
And then the policy question becomes a very
interesting one, but not one I'm an expert

00:10:58.780 --> 00:10:59.780
on.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:00.780
So I'm going to finesse it.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:04.590
Speaker 1: Well, no, but I guess I mean, from
from the standpoint that is more more relevant

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:10.200
to your work, there is that question of when
we say heritability, is it genetically heritable

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.210
versus is it culturally heritable?

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:19.320
My instinct would be that and that while there
may be a genetic heritability, if you take

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.010
a group of kids that are statistically more
likely to be Republicans from Utah or something

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:29.520
like that, but you raise them in liberal Massachusetts
families, my instinct is that the upbringing

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.070
will have a significant impact even on whatever
genetic heritability is there.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:35.070
I don't know.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:36.070
I'm thinking out loud.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:37.070
Speaker 5: Yeah.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.070
David, I agree with you.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:44.140
It's always nurture as well as nature and
how kids are brought up or the culture you're

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:53.450
socialized into has a great impact on how
you behave, even given, you know, X, Y, Z,

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.630
inherited tendency.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:57.800
So I, I'm with you.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.130
I think it would matter, but we don't know.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:07.560
Speaker 1: Do you, um, do you take an evolutionary
perspective at all on human empathy?

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:15.620
Because I've read a lot one way or the other
about the evolutionary advantage of empathy.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:19.900
And there are there's the position that it's
sort of vestigial, that that an advantage

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.390
that was at one point relevant may no longer
be today.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:24.390
I don't know.

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:25.390
Is that something you think about?

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:30.000
Speaker 5: Well, I tend to take an evolutionary
perspective.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.670
And from my from that point of view, empathy
has a real upside, because when humans started

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:45.680
living in groups and cooperating, which takes
empathy, teamwork basically made humans the

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.660
species that was a standout.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:55.590
Regrettably, evolution did not foresee the
predicament we're in now where our economics,

00:12:55.590 --> 00:13:03.970
the everyday things we buy and use, are degrading
the ecosystem we depend on to survive.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:09.560
And I think that will become a more and more
compelling reality for younger generations.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:18.980
As time goes on, I think it will reverberate
back into the business world and that corporations

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.310
companies will be fine.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:27.350
It is in their best interest to do things
in a better way for the environment because

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:29.620
they want to keep market share and people
will care.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:36.751
Speaker 1: Do you think that there's or is
there a particular lens that you think is

00:13:36.751 --> 00:13:41.800
a valuable lens to think about the issues
you work on that's essentially missing right

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.940
now from the national conversation?

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:50.260
In other words, if you are sort of going to
the typical news sources and informed by what

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:56.130
people see on social media, some some aspect
of thinking about the society in which we

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.580
now live, that's not really getting the attention
it deserves.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:02.020
That you think would be a great thing if it
did.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:08.870
Speaker 5: I call it radical transparency
and I mean ecological transparency.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.110
And I actually I just did a podcast on this.

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:15.150
It's an idea who I think whose time has come.

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:20.279
Basically right now we have full transparency
about the cost of products.

00:14:20.279 --> 00:14:24.110
We have zero transparency about the ecological
impacts of products.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:32.540
So if you were to buy a new mike or a new
shirt, ideally, and the methodology for this

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:41.120
exists, you could know the full environmental
impact of that over its full lifecycle.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:48.220
And you would know that a point of purchase
this this is the ideal and it might happen

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:51.790
one day and I think it may be expedited by
younger generations.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:15:01.029
The reason being the coming climate crisis,
which is being the engine of which has been

00:15:01.029 --> 00:15:02.160
our economic system.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.870
Speaker 1: Let's see if we can do an example
of that.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:08.560
So we know that a lot of clothing is now really
cheap.

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:15.060
And we intuitively know that part of the reason
it's inexpensive is it's produced in countries

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:19.540
with very that pay very low wages.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:23.820
And that is why we are able to get a shirt
for seven dollars that maybe would otherwise

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:25.120
cost thirty dollars.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.671
The idea of the transparency you're talking
about would be when you see the seven dollar

00:15:29.671 --> 00:15:35.630
price tag of the Sri Lankan made shirt, it
might have something there that says the full

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:39.950
product life cycle makes this really a 40
dollars shirt.

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:46.190
Now, I, as the buyer, may not be out of pocket
for the additional thirty three dollars, but

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:51.690
it would at least be available to me to know
that that cost is part of this purchase.

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:56.090
And it may be more expensive than the 30 dollar
US made shirt.

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:59.460
I mean, in theory, to come up with just an
example of what you mean.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:05.860
Speaker 5: Yeah, so I would I think that the
ecological transparency would probably be

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:11.410
more powerful when you're choosing between
a seven dollar and a ten dollars shirt.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:20.240
And you find out that the ten dollars shirt
is made with alternative energy and is made

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:26.830
with cotton, which is not grown in a place
where water is super scarce and it's driving

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:29.170
people away because of that.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:34.150
And the factory that it was made in actually
pays a decent wage rather than starvation

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:35.270
wage.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:40.190
And you may think, well, you know, I'm going
to do some good with that extra three bucks

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:43.240
and I'm going to buy the ten dollar shirt
rather than the seven dollar shirt.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:49.990
And I think that will start to show that transparency
would shift market share so that the guy who's

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:55.430
making the seven dollars shirt and say, hey,
why aren't people buying my shirts and find

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:59.800
out and say, OK, well, I'm going to do this
and this and this and then get certified by

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:05.879
an independent body that he's improved and
then he's now again competitive with the ten

00:17:05.879 --> 00:17:06.879
dollars.

00:17:06.879 --> 00:17:07.879
Speaker 1: That's interesting.

00:17:07.879 --> 00:17:10.980
I mean, I think you're right that the comparison
is much easier.

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:14.179
The decision is much easier when it's seven
versus ten.

00:17:14.179 --> 00:17:17.770
But maybe that's some of the difficulty as
well, because I don't you know, I don't know

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:21.760
enough about the production of shirts to know
this, but it's conceivable that the difference

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:23.269
would be far bigger.

00:17:23.269 --> 00:17:30.249
And so the the financial impact on an individual
might be less trivial than maybe seven to

00:17:30.249 --> 00:17:31.470
ten dollars might be.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:36.910
Speaker 5: I actually think that in in the
actual competitive arena of companies who

00:17:36.910 --> 00:17:43.019
are looking at their supply chain, how they
extract, how they manufacture, they would

00:17:43.019 --> 00:17:45.990
do incremental changes.

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:51.740
And so, you know, the two seven dollar shirts
would say, hey, my civilian shirt doesn't

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:55.559
isn't going to threaten the climate.

00:17:55.559 --> 00:17:56.559
That guys is right.

00:17:56.559 --> 00:18:00.669
In other words, that's more likely to be the
arena.

00:18:00.669 --> 00:18:05.480
And, you know, another interesting example
is our cell phones.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:09.159
Everybody's got one these days and.

00:18:09.159 --> 00:18:14.159
Who knew that they all have rare earth, as
they're called, some of which are mined in

00:18:14.159 --> 00:18:15.750
places that have slave labor?

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:16.750
Yes.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:22.610
Yes, well, darn, you know, what are you going
to do about that if we had transparency about

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:23.610
that?

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.110
I think that that could change.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:31.210
Yeah, a lot of ways in which transparency
and it's really bizarre because one of the

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:37.420
fundamental principles of the free market
economy is transparency.

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:38.769
And there is zero transparency.

00:18:38.769 --> 00:18:44.110
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah, I think, you know,
the electric car example is an interesting

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:51.820
one because I think this is being improved
upon, but the footprint of making the batteries

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.749
is not insignificant.

00:18:53.749 --> 00:18:58.710
And then what you do with the batteries after
the fact is I think we're closer to solving

00:18:58.710 --> 00:18:59.710
that.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:01.539
But the disposal is also significant.

00:19:01.539 --> 00:19:06.419
And that is is completely opaque, I think,
to most most electric car buyers.

00:19:06.419 --> 00:19:08.980
Speaker 5: And plastics is the same.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:14.309
If you were to do a lifecycle assessment of
plastic and that's what this method is called,

00:19:14.309 --> 00:19:19.940
it would look like plastic bags are better
than brown paper bags, but that's because

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:23.169
the lifecycle assessment does not include
end of life.

00:19:23.169 --> 00:19:24.169
Right.

00:19:24.169 --> 00:19:31.039
And plastics, as we now are finding out, are
in us and fish and birds and everything else.

00:19:31.039 --> 00:19:33.620
And it's a huge problem that we had not realized.

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.759
So anyway, you asked what's missing and I
think that's what's missing.

00:19:37.759 --> 00:19:39.149
Speaker 1: That's a great example.

00:19:39.149 --> 00:19:42.200
Yeah, that's a that's a beautiful note to
to to end on.

